# Function naming: `snake_case` (new trees)

Applies to **new** C++ under **`core/`** wrappers, **`algorithm/`**, and new modules under **`src/`** (and any future `base/` / `gfx/` / `gui/`). Does **not** apply to `third_party/` or **`branches/`** 2010–2013 sources (keep `GetLength` / `Set` ABI).

## Must be `snake_case` (new code)

Free functions, member functions (including `static`), and any **named** callable the project owns (e.g. named function objects used as API).

```cpp
// BAD
void LoadConfig();
int shardCount() const;

// GOOD
void load_config();
int shard_count() const;
```

Boolean predicates prefer readable prefixes: `is_` / `has_` / `can_` / `should_`.

## Rename policy (when editing)

If new or touched code **in scope** encounters a project-owned function named in `camelCase` or `PascalCase`:

1. Rename it to **`snake_case`** in the **declaration and definition**.
2. Update **all in-tree call sites of that new-tree symbol** in the **same change**.
3. Do **not** leave a local-only rename or a dual-name shim unless an external ABI forces it (then document why next to the symbol).

Do **not** drive-by rename unrelated symbols outside the edit’s blast radius; do rename every use of a symbol you are already changing.

Do **not** rename **`Smt_*` / MFC / LoadLibrary** exports or **`branches/`** ABI to snake_case.

## Out of scope (do not snake_case)

- **Types**: `class` / `struct` / `enum` / type aliases stay **PascalCase** (or match existing type style in that file).
- **mojom / protobuf generated** symbols and names dictated by `.mojom` / `.proto`.
- **Third-party / STL / Win32 / MFC** names you call or override when the external name is fixed.
- Wrapper **entry points that must match an external ABI or virtual override** you cannot change — keep the required name; prefer a thin `snake_case` internal helper when practical.
- Legacy exports under `src/` (2010–2013 ABI).

## Allowed exceptions

- `operator…` overloads.
- Single-word STL-style members already conventional (`begin`, `end`, `size`, `empty`, `data`, `clear`, `swap`, `reset`) when matching container/iterator conventions.
- Macros and test macros (`TEST`, `TEST_F`, `BENCHMARK`, etc.).

## Relation to other rules

**`.cursor/rules/style/tree-style.mdc`** (local consistency), **`.cursor/rules/style/namespace.mdc`** (namespace layers), **`.cursor/rules/style/cxx-standard.mdc`** (C++23 + traits). Narrower product rules may add more naming detail; they must **not** weaken this rule inside new trees.
